<CDName>giac_procedures1</CDName>
<CDBase>https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd</CDBase>
<CDURL>https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd/giac_procedures1.ocd</CDURL>
<CDReviewDate>2027-11-28</CDReviewDate>
<CDStatus>private</CDStatus>
<CDDate>2026-11-28</CDDate>
<CDVersion>0</CDVersion>
<CDRevision>1</CDRevision>

<Description>
Requests to the GIAC computer algebra system (https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~parisse/giac.html),
made SCSCP-style with scscp1.procedure_call (with or without SCSCP's session
attributes such as call_id).
The symbols of this CD name requests, not mathematical objects: what GIAC
answers depends on its version and settings. They belong in a procedure_call
only; a result, a claim or evidence is written in official symbols, with its
relation to the request explicit: equal where defined under the premises, a
list of solutions (not implied complete), agreement to an order, an
approximation (checkable only with an enclosure). Readers refuse these symbols elsewhere, and refuse this
CD unless their caller enables it.
</Description>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>simplify</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's simplify command, simplify(e). GIAC returns a form of e GIAC considers simpler. GIAC intends a form equal to e wherever e is defined under the premises of the call. This is not guaranteed (GIAC may cancel a factor without saying that e is undefined where it vanished): it is the relation a result is checked against, equality where defined.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="simplify"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="divide"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="minus"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="power"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>2</OMI></OMA><OMI>1</OMI></OMA><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="minus"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>1</OMI></OMA></OMA></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>expand</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's expand command, expand(e). GIAC returns e with its products and powers expanded. GIAC intends a form equal to e wherever e is defined under the premises of the call. This is not guaranteed (GIAC may cancel a factor without saying that e is undefined where it vanished): it is the relation a result is checked against, equality where defined.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="expand"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="power"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="plus"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>1</OMI></OMA><OMI>3</OMI></OMA></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>factor</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's factor command, factor(e). GIAC returns e factored. The coefficient field is the rationals, or the complex numbers when the call's complex_mode is true; the premises play no part in it. GIAC intends a form equal to e wherever e is defined under the premises of the call. This is not guaranteed (GIAC may cancel a factor without saying that e is undefined where it vanished): it is the relation a result is checked against, equality where defined.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="factor"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="minus"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="power"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>4</OMI></OMA><OMI>1</OMI></OMA></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>solve</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's solve command, solve(r, v). GIAC returns a list of values of the variable v (a list of lists for several variables) for the relation r, or a list of relations. Each listed value is a solution of r under the premises, which can be checked by substitution; the list is not implied to be complete (GIAC may give the solutions in one period only), unless GIAC says so.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="solve"/><OMA><OMS cd="relation1" name="eq"/><OMA><OMS cd="transc1" name="sin"/><OMV name="x"/></OMA><OMI>0</OMI></OMA><OMV name="x"/></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>evalf</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's evalf command, evalf(e) or evalf(e, d). GIAC returns a floating-point approximation of e, with d significant digits if d is given. It is an approximation, never an equality. Without an error bound it can be reported but not checked; a checkable claim needs an enclosure of e and its width.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="evalf"/><OMS cd="nums1" name="pi"/><OMI>30</OMI></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>series</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's series command, series(e, v, a, n). GIAC returns the series expansion of e in v at a, with its terms up to (v - a)^n, followed by GIAC's order term for the rest. No official symbol writes the order term: a result is the polynomial part, and the relation is that it agrees with e to order n at a, the difference being O((v - a)^(n+1)).
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="series"/><OMA><OMS cd="transc1" name="sin"/><OMV name="x"/></OMA><OMV name="x"/><OMI>0</OMI><OMI>4</OMI></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>desolve</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's desolve command, desolve(r, y). GIAC returns the solutions of the differential equation r for the unknown function y, as GIAC returns them. They introduce new constants (GIAC names them c_0, c_1, ...): a result's constants are new names, bound by the result (each value of them giving a solution), which a reader must not take for names of the request.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and on the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. Its arguments are OpenMath
objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the call are
the semantic attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="desolve"/><OMA><OMS cd="relation1" name="eq"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="plus"/><OMA><OMA><OMS cd="calculus1" name="diff"/><OMBIND><OMS cd="fns1" name="lambda"/><OMBVAR><OMV name="x"/></OMBVAR><OMA><OMV name="y"/><OMV name="x"/></OMA></OMBIND></OMA><OMV name="x"/></OMA><OMA><OMV name="y"/><OMV name="x"/></OMA></OMA><OMI>0</OMI></OMA><OMV name="y"/></OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>premises</Name>
<Role>semantic-attribution</Role>
<Description>
The assumptions GIAC makes during the call (GIAC's assume, for the call only), as a boolean OpenMath object in official symbols: a relation, logic1.and or logic1.or of relations, or set1.in(x, setname1.R) to declare a variable real. The answer of the call holds under them. A semantic attribution: it changes the meaning of the request, and a reader that does not understand it refuses the call rather than drop it.
</Description>
<Example>
A complete request, SCSCP-style: scscp1's call_id, option_return_object and
option_runtime on the procedure_call application, as scscp1 places them; the
premises and the complex mode on its argument, since they qualify the
mathematical question.
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMATTR>
    <OMATP>
      <OMS cd="scscp1" name="call_id"/> <OMSTR>example-1</OMSTR>
      <OMS cd="scscp1" name="option_return_object"/> <OMSTR></OMSTR>
      <OMS cd="scscp1" name="option_runtime"/> <OMI>5000</OMI>
    </OMATP>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
      <OMATTR>
        <OMATP>
          <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="premises"/>
          <OMA><OMS cd="relation1" name="gt"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>0</OMI></OMA>
          <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="complex_mode"/>
          <OMS cd="logic1" name="false"/>
        </OMATP>
        <OMA><OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="simplify"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="root"/><OMA><OMS cd="arith1" name="power"/><OMV name="x"/><OMI>2</OMI></OMA><OMI>2</OMI></OMA></OMA>
      </OMATTR>
    </OMA>
  </OMATTR>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>complex_mode</Name>
<Role>semantic-attribution</Role>
<Description>
Whether GIAC computes over the complex numbers during the call (GIAC's complex_mode): logic1.true or logic1.false. It sets, for instance, the coefficient field of factor. It does not declare variables real or complex: premises do (set1.in(x, setname1.R)). A semantic attribution, like premises.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>
</CD>
